WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.110
Hey it's Michelle from Lab Muffin
Beauty Science the nerdiest beauty nerd

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:09.030
on your screen right now. Today I'm going
to be discussing how to get vitamin D

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:19.050
without giving yourself too much skin
damage. Vitamin D is an essential

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:23.039
nutrient for your body and one of the
main sources of vitamin D for humans

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:29.670
is exposing our skin to UVB radiation
from the Sun. But of course UV and sun

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:34.020
exposure also causes skin cancer and
skin aging, so we need to be careful

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.370
about sun protection - we need sunscreen
hats protective clothing and we need to

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:43.110
remember to stay in the shade. So what's
the right balance between getting enough

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:48.660
sun for vitamin D and not having skin
cancer? This topic is quite controversial

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.570
and there's tons and tons of opinions on
the internet.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:56.610
They aren't many clear guidelines from
medical authorities either. So here's my

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:05.000
best attempt at untangling the
information that we have.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.140
Vitamin D is an important vitamin that
has lots of roles in your body. It's

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.820
famous for maintaining calcium so your
bones don't get soft and you avoid

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.210
osteoporosis, but it's involved in lots
of other things as well like heart

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:22.910
disease, metabolism and preventing cancer.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:30.750
Your skin produces vitamin D when it's
exposed to short wavelengths of UV

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:37.560
called UVB. It happens with wavelengths
around 270 to 300 nanometers and it

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:44.520
happens the most efficiently at about
295 nanometers. The amount of vitamin D

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.210
that your body produces when you go out
into the Sun depends on lots of things

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:53.520
like where you live, the time of year, the
time of day, and the color of your skin.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.350
As well as making vitamin D in your skin
you can also get vitamin D from your

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:03.899
diet - things like fatty fish liver cheese
mushrooms and foods that are specially

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:06.360
modified so that there's vitamin D added
to it.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.890
All of these can increase your body's
vitamin D, but in general food only

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:16.200
contributes about 10 percent of your
total needed vitamin D. You can also

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:21.420
take vitamin D supplements. A lot of
people are vitamin D deficient so they

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.769
aren't getting enough through the Sun
and food - it's something like a quarter

00:02:25.769 --> 00:02:35.030
of Australians and 1/3 of people in the
US who are all vitamin D deficient.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:39.720
Sunscreen blocks a lot of UVB from
entering your skin so it seems logical

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:45.630
to think that wearing sunscreens is
going to lower your vitamin D. But a 2009

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:49.050
study found that wearing sunscreen
doesn't actually have too much of an

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:53.640
effect on your vitamin D levels. That's
because most people don't apply enough

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.440
sunscreen and even if you try to apply
the right amount there are lots of spots

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:03.060
that you miss like your scalp, between
your fingers, around your eyes, and these

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:08.310
spots get enough UV to make vitamin D.
There's an interesting idea that you can

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:13.140
make a sunscreen that blocks most UV but
selectively lets in wavelengths of UV

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.940
that your skin uses to make vitamin D.
The only sunscreen that I know off the

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.110
top of my head that does this is called
Solar D, and it lets in some rays that

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:27.330
are between 290 and 310 nanometers.
I personally think this sort of

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:35.000
sunscreen is really going to take off in
the near future.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.330
It's hard to give an exact figure in
terms of how many minutes of Sun

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:42.450
exposure you should get because of all
those things that I mentioned before

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:46.350
that will affect your vitamin D
production - so things like where you live,

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:50.430
the time of year, the time of day and
what color your skin is.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:54.180
But there are some calculations that
have been done on the minimal

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:59.790
erythemal dose or MED which is the
minimum amount of UV that makes your

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:04.530
skin go pink. There's research that found
that exposing about a quarter of your

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:10.170
body's skin surface to half of the MED
two or three times a week is enough to

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:15.510
get adequate vitamin D synthesis. You
also don't need to seem to need Sun all

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:20.489
that regularly for vitamin D - your body
can rely on its storages of vitamin D

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:23.970
for a couple of months so you might be
able to get through some of winter

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:33.090
without sunshine. Different countries
have put out different guidelines for

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:36.450
sun exposure and vitamin D
supplementation so that you can get

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:41.040
enough vitamin D without risking too
much skin damage. In the US there

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.360
aren't any broad guidelines yet. Some
scientist suggests that around winter,

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.740
you should try to get about 15 minutes
of Sun two or three times a week with

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:54.270
your face, arms and legs exposed. They
recommend that you do this in the middle

00:04:54.270 --> 00:05:00.660
of the day when the UVB is highest so
around 11 a.m. till 3 p.m. This

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:04.950
guideline is for people with light
Fitzpatrick II skin so if your skin

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:09.210
tone's lighter then you need less sun, and
if your skin tone's darker you're going

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:13.560
to need more. I really like the
guidelines given in Australia because

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:17.250
they're really clear and they were put
together in collaboration between people

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:20.580
who think that the Sun is bad,
so the Australasian College of

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:25.140
Dermatologists and the Cancer Council, as
well as the people who think that

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:29.640
vitamin D deficiency is really bad, so
the Australian and New Zealand Bone and

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:34.950
Mineral Society, the Endocrine Society of
Australia and Osteoporosis Australia.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:40.800
These guidelines are also really recent -
they were put together in January 2016,

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.990
so they take into account newer research
on vitamin D's

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:50.990
importance. These guidelines say when the
UV index is 3 or above in the middle

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.640
of the day, which is most of the year in
Australia, sun protection is recommended

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:00.080
when you're outdoors for more than a few
minutes. So during this time most

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.280
Australians will still get enough
vitamin D from incidental exposure even

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:07.820
though they're taking sun protection
measures - so even though they're wearing

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:12.920
sunscreen, wearing sunglasses, wearing
protective clothing, wearing hats. Walking

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:16.670
between the car and the shops, going
outside to buy lunch, that sort of

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:22.310
thing will give you enough sunshine to
get vitamin D. Then in late autumn and

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:27.590
winter when the UV index is below 3,
sun protection is not recommended and

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:32.300
they say that people should go outdoors
in the middle of the day with some skin

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:37.730
uncovered on most days of the week. So
that's really nice and clear, and because

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:41.480
it's based on the UV index you can
probably apply that in other places in

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:43.900
the world - just check the UV index for your

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:48.160
location and you can work out what to do.
There are some apps that you can use to

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:51.440
do this, so for example in Australia
there's the SunSmart app from the

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.900
Cancer Council. In the UK it's often
cloudy and there's low UVB so in winter

00:06:56.900 --> 00:07:01.550
the NHS recommends that almost everyone
should consider taking a vitamin D

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:05.780
supplement. I've put links to all this
advice in the caption if you want to

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:11.539
read them in full.

00:07:11.539 --> 00:07:16.169
So in addition to this advice there's a
few things that I want to add that you

00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:24.719
should keep in mind. Sun exposure isn't
just UVB exposure.

00:07:24.719 --> 00:07:29.430
There's also longer wavelength UVA which
doesn't have any health benefits and

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:34.979
there's also infrared and visible light
that can also cause skin damage. UVB is

00:07:34.979 --> 00:07:38.189
highest in the middle of the day and
changes quite a lot during the day so

00:07:38.189 --> 00:07:42.599
it's quite low in the morning and in the
evening, but UVA levels are pretty

00:07:42.599 --> 00:07:46.919
constant all throughout the day.
So in terms of cost and benefit the middle

00:07:46.919 --> 00:07:50.969
of the day seems to be the best time to
get the maximum vitamin D synthesis in

00:07:50.969 --> 00:07:56.150
your skin while minimising skin damage
from other sources.

00:07:58.169 --> 00:08:03.999
You only really need short exposures to
sunshine to make vitamin D. It doesn't

00:08:03.999 --> 00:08:08.050
mean that you have to bake in the Sun
for hours - your skin slows down how much

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:12.909
vitamin D it makes after a while but
skin damage doesn't really slow down, so

00:08:12.909 --> 00:08:17.529
you're risking melanoma and other skin
cancers, especially if you get sunburned

00:08:17.529 --> 00:08:26.709
while you're trying to get vitamin D. UVB
doesn't pass through glass, so if you're

00:08:26.709 --> 00:08:30.249
sitting in a patch of sunshine that's
coming in through a window you're not

00:08:30.249 --> 00:08:36.550
going to make much vitamin D. However UVA -
those longer wavelengths - they do pass

00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:44.829
through glass which means you'll still
get skin damage. Tanning beds also

00:08:44.829 --> 00:08:50.560
produce way more UVA than UVB so they're
worse than regular sunlight and they've

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:54.990
been definitely linked to skin cancer.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:02.080
If you're using anti-aging ingredients
in your skincare like alpha hydroxy

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:06.400
acids and vitamin A, you should still
wear sunscreen since a lot of these can

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:16.030
increase your risk of sun damage. If you
can't follow these guidelines or you're

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:20.410
particularly at risk of vitamin D
deficiency like if you or your family

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:24.400
members have had skin cancer, if your
skin is really dark or if you're house

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.870
bound, or if you're just concerned about
your vitamin D levels in general, then

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:32.710
you should talk to your doctor about
checking your vitamin D levels, and talk

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:36.910
about other options for increasing your
vitamin D. So for example perhaps you can

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:46.540
take a supplement. In summary small
periods of sun exposure in the middle of

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:50.680
the day will give you vitamin D while
minimising your chances of skin cancer

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:56.800
and aging. Sunburn and UVA are still both
really bad and sunscreen is really

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:00.480
unlikely to make you more vitamin D
deficient than you already are.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:05.170
Thanks for watching this video if you
liked my video I'd love it if you could

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:09.220
subscribe to my channel. You can also
follow me on Facebook and Instagram for

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:13.270
lots and lots of science-based beauty
tips. And you can also check out my blog

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:16.990
if you want to read more about beauty
science. See you next time for more

